Calculations
Average distance:
412.1 cm = 4.121 m
Average flight time:
1.878 s ≈ 1.88 s
Longest distance: 470 cm
Shortest distance: 328 cm
Longest flight time: 2.47 s
Shortest flight time: 1.38 s

Result
SUCCESSFUL ✅
The redesigned glider was able to maintain flight and produced measurable flight distances and times.

Observation 
I noticed that the gilder flew for a longer time when the wings were lifted in test 1 and 2.
When I increased the launch force (thrust) it flew a longer distance in a shorter time seen in test 5 and 6


Conclusion
The second design performed better than my first design. Increasing the wing area and improving the structure helped the aircraft glide successfully.
However, the results varied between flights. This may have been caused by differences in launch angle, launch force, wind conditions, or small changes in the aircraft's orientation.
What I learned
I learned that aeronautical engineering involves testing, measuring, analysing and improving designs. A failed prototype is useful because it tells me what needs to change.
🧠 FINAL PROJECT SUMMARY
My first paper-glider project taught me that engineering is a process of designing, testing, failing, learning and improving. 
Glider A was unsuccessful, but instead of giving up, I changed the design and developed Glider B, which successfully flew.
I then conducted 10 controlled flight tests and collected measurable data. 
This project introduced me to concepts such as lift, stability, wing area, flight time, distance and experimental testing.
